2.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {B remains the clearest violation. The ESA distribution duty is absolute and ongoing: any required policy must be given to every employee (including new hires) within 30 days, with no statutory carve-out for cost or administrative burden. Employer B’s explicit refusal on cost grounds therefore places it out of compliance on 2 January 2023 regardless of when the policies were first written.  

By contrast, C’s monthly updates followed by quarterly redistribution still falls inside the 30-day window because each revision is issued before the prior 30-day clock expires; the quarterly cadence simply batches the already-timely notices. This satisfies the letter of the rule.  

Options A, D and E never crossed the 25-employee threshold on the controlling January 1 snapshot dates, so no policy obligation ever attached. Option D’s later head-count drop is irrelevant once the 1 January 2022 count locked in the electronic-monitoring duty, but the employer in D did maintain the disconnecting policy and the scenario gives no indication it failed the distribution requirement for that policy. Thus only B is demonstrably non-compliant.}

14.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {B remains the clearest case of non-compliance. The ESA distribution obligation is triggered once an employer voluntarily maintains written policies on disconnecting from work and electronic monitoring; copies must reach every employee—including new hires—within 30 days, with no statutory carve-out for cost or administrative burden. Refusing distribution outright therefore violates the rule regardless of the 23-employee headcount.  

C’s situation is different but still problematic: monthly policy updates followed by quarterly redistribution necessarily exceeds the 30-day window for revised copies, creating an ongoing breach for a 1,000-employee employer that is indisputably subject to both obligations. The snapshot rule and head-count changes cited for D do not erase the prior-year duty, yet D at least possesses one policy and faces no explicit refusal-to-distribute issue.  

Options A and E stayed below the threshold on every relevant January 1 date, so no policies were ever required. By contrast, B’s deliberate non-distribution is a direct, ongoing violation that cannot be excused.}

7.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {B remains the clearest case of non-compliance. The ESA distribution rule is absolute once policies exist: every employee, including new hires, must receive a copy within 30 days regardless of cost or timing of hire. B's explicit refusal on cost grounds violates that duty outright. 

Col1's reading of C is understandable but overstates the breach. Monthly updates followed by quarterly redistribution can still fall inside the 30-day window if the formal "preparation or revision" date for each policy is treated as the quarterly issuance point rather than each incremental monthly tweak; nothing in the scenario shows a policy sitting unrevised for more than 30 days before distribution. That leaves C compliant on a reasonable construction of the timing rule.

By contrast, B's refusal is unambiguous and ongoing. Options A, D and E stay below the 25-employee January 1 snapshot on every relevant year, so no policies are required at all. D's later head-count drop does not erase the obligation that attached on 1 Jan 2022, but the scenario already shows D has the disconnect policy and the electronic-monitoring deadline had not yet produced a clear violation on 2 Jan 2023. Only B is actively breaching a distribution duty it chose to assume.}

