PRIVATE REFLECTION: {Majority (now ~70% of colleagues) converged on D as sole clear violation: 30 employees on 1 Jan 2022 snapshot triggered mandatory electronic-monitoring policy (deadline 11 Oct 2022) + disconnecting-from-work policy; D has only the latter. Snapshot rule is one-way—later drop to 24 does not extinguish the duty. B (23 employees) and A/E (below threshold on both 2022/2023 snapshots) owe no statutory duties at all, so refusals or absences are irrelevant. C satisfies creation/distribution on reasonable reading of 30-day window (quarterly batches after monthly revisions still timely).

Minority (B advocates: 13,14,16,17,21,2,5,7,9) argue distribution duty is absolute/ongoing once policies exist, with no cost exception; some also flag C’s quarterly cadence as exceeding 30 days. These positions ignore that below-threshold employers create no enforceable obligation.

Undisputed preserved facts: 25+ threshold measured strictly on prior 1 Jan; distribution to all (incl. new hires) within 30 days of creation/revision; no cost carve-out. Unresolved: whether voluntary policies below threshold can create distribution duty; precise “prepared or revised” trigger date for incremental monthly updates. No primary statutory text cited; D remains the unambiguous non-compliance on 2 Jan 2023.}

