9.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {B is still the clearest and most unambiguous case of non-compliance. The ESA distribution obligation is triggered the moment an employer prepares written policies on disconnecting from work or electronic monitoring, and it requires every employee—including new hires—to receive a copy within 30 days of any creation or revision. The statute contains no exception for cost, administrative burden, or “existing employees only,” so B’s explicit refusal on cost grounds is a direct, ongoing breach as of 2 January 2023.  

C’s monthly updates followed by quarterly redistribution necessarily exceeds the 30-day window for at least some revisions, creating a secondary but still material timing violation for an employer that indisputably meets the 25-employee threshold.  

By contrast, the snapshot rule fixes obligations only for employers that crossed 25 employees on the controlling January 1 date; employers below that line (A, D after the drop, E) never incurred statutory duties, and any voluntary policies they chose to maintain do not retroactively impose the same mandatory distribution clock. D supporters correctly note the permanence of a triggered obligation, yet D at least possesses the disconnecting-from-work policy and faces no outright refusal-to-distribute issue. B’s deliberate non-distribution therefore stands alone as the clearest statutory violation on the facts given.}

